  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1017 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nThe middle of the week may be the problem child of this forecast. \nMost, if not all, medium range guidance suggests a strong, amplified \nupper ridge developing over the southeast U.S. This will be the \nculprit in pushing afternoon highs back into the middle (and some \nupper) 90s Tuesday and Wednesday. Dew points should be such that \nwe'll be very close to Heat Advisory criteria both days. By \nThursday, the ridge will start to weaken and temperatures could be a \ntouch cooler, but we'll run near 10 degrees above average through \nthe middle of the week.\n\nFriday should be the start of *some* relief. Temperatures in our \nextended guidance are expected to back off to where they currently \nare and rain chances should return to the forecast. Now, HOW they \nreturn is the question. Latest GFS suggests a front sweeping through \nthe region on Friday. Euro has more upper level troughiness without \na frontal passage. Obviously there's plenty of details to work out \nhere, but what each is showing is large upper troughing over the \neastern half of the U.S. in the 7-10 day range. Could this be our \nfirst break in the heat in quite some time?",
